#open-question(title: [Does H1 Blockade Redistribute Free Histamine onto Immunosuppressive H2 Receptors?])[
Histamine enhances Th1 responses through H1 receptors but suppresses IL-12 and stimulates IL-10 through H2 receptors, biasing T cells toward a Th2/anti-inflammatory profile @Jutel2001H1H2Tcell, @Elenkov1998HistamineIL12IL10, @Packard2003HistamineTh1Th2. The receptor-differential model is consolidated in the authoritative review @Jutel2009HistamineImmune. If H1 is blocked, free histamine can bind only the unblocked H2 receptor, so a net Th2 shift (a raised Th2/Th1 ratio) becomes mechanistically plausible. This means H1 monotherapy could be immunologically counterproductive in a mast-cell-driven state, whereas combined H1+H2 blockade would prevent the redistribution. Whether the redistribution actually occurs at clinical antihistamine concentrations in MCAS/ME/CFS — and whether any resulting Th2 bias is clinically meaningful rather than an in vitro artefact — is unresolved: the receptor model is well established, but the redistribution-to-Th2 consequence has never been measured in a treated patient cohort.

This is an open question; no certainty is assigned. The receptor-level mechanism is established in vitro and in knockout mice, but the redistribution consequence in treated humans is untested.

*Falsifiable prediction:* In MCAS/ME/CFS patients, H1 monotherapy raises the Th2/Th1 cytokine ratio (for example IL-4/IFN-γ or IL-10/IL-12) relative to baseline, whereas combined H1+H2 blockade leaves the ratio unchanged; if H1 monotherapy does not shift the ratio, the redistribution mechanism is not clinically operative.

*Consequence:* If real, the finding would overturn the default assumption that antihistamine monotherapy is immunologically inert and would argue for H1+H2 co-blockade whenever H1 blockade is used in mast-cell-driven illness — but until the shift is measured, H1 monotherapy remains the clinical default and no patient-management change is justified.
] <oq:histamine-h1-h2-redistribution>
